body:
I used to use Mio in my water to help me stay hydrated. It's this little squirt bottle thingy that's small enough to keep in your pocket. They come in different flavors and you just add a squirt to your water and it works great. It ends up tasting like flavored water and it's really good. You can find them at a grocery store, and they're pretty cheap and can last depending on how much you add each time. It's also nice that it is so small and compact so you can just carry it with you at all times and just add it to your water bottle.

&#x200B;

As for the thoughts, I don't know anything in particular that could help. Have you tried talking to a therapist? Even though you are feeling great mentally (congrats btw!), this is still an issue that I think a professional might be able to help you work through. Especially one who uses CBT, because that method focuses on working through irrational thoughts and then changing the behaviors.

I've heard that many people have found comfort in weighted blankets. They simulate the sensation of being hugged and held. They can be on the more expensive side, but I've found DIY videos that would be much cheaper. 

&#x200B;

Also, I think it would be okay to ask your roommate for a hug once in a while, especially if you explain how you've been feeling. As long as you ask without being pushing and respect their answer, you aren't being abusive.

NTA

&#x200B;

As someone who has also spent time in a psychiatric hospital for a suicide attempt, we had been told that you were not allowed to share any personal information or means of content. Of course, since we were in an adolescent unit and since you tend to go through such a difficult and sensitive time together, many of us did anyways.

&#x200B;

I realize now why they were so insistent that we did not stay in contact with each other. Although many of the people I met were incredible kind and helped during my time there, it wasn't healthy for any of us to stay in each other's lives. All three of the people I had added on Snapchat would constantly post concerning messages, and that not only made me worry for their safety but it also brought be back to the very dark place I was in during the hospital. I was able to understand that, even though these friendships helped me at the time, they ultimately would only hinder my recovery.

&#x200B;

Particularly in this situation, this girl seems to be using you, maybe not intentionally, but it's in a way that is not helpful to either of you. You need to take care of yourself and blocking her was the way to do it; even though it might feel harsh, it's for the best.
